<div class="page">
  <h1 class="page-title">About me</h1>
  <p class="page-lede">
    I build things with AI agents to find out where they actually hold up. Most of it is about
    making learning fun and open to anyone who wants it.
  </p>
  <div class="page-rule"></div>

  <div class="pc-prose">
    <p>
      Most of what gets said about AI comes from people selling something. I'm not selling
      anything. I'd rather find out for myself, so I build real projects with it and write down
      what broke. That's what this site is for.
    </p>

    <h2>What I'm making</h2>
    <p>
      The big one right now is <strong>I Love My Evening Television</strong>,
      a series of half-hour films made by crews of Claude agents from one prompt each. Every episode
      starts as a calm little programme and turns strange at minute 20. The first two are on
      YouTube, and <a href="/courses/i-love-my-evening-television/">the lore course</a>
      keeps track of the whole world.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Hidden Curriculum</strong> is a free video course
      on the small stuff nobody tells you when you start building with AI, like what a folder path
      is. Agents write, fact-check, narrate and animate every lesson.
      <a href="/courses/the-hidden-curriculum/">Start it here</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then there are my original characters. <strong>Jefrie</strong>,
      <strong>BloodTailor</strong> and the rest of the cast keep
      showing up in everything I make. They get drawn, painted in oil, animated and dropped into games.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the game side there's <strong>BLACKWATER</strong>, zombies
      but you're cave diving, which I built in a weekend and you can play in your browser. And
      <strong>LLM Monster Hunter</strong>, a monster-catching game
      where no monster exists until you meet it. The AI writes, paints and referees each one while
      the code keeps every number. It's paused for now. Everything else is on
      <a href="/projects">the projects page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I write it down</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/ledger">The ledger</a> is where I file what I tried and what came of it,
      dead ends included. The prompt that seemed obviously right and wasn't. The agent that did
      something so strange I had to screenshot it. If you're learning this stuff too, I think an
      honest write-up of a failure is worth more than another launch announcement.
    </p>

    <h2>Away from the keyboard</h2>
    <p>
      I'm learning Japanese and I volunteer with a local Japanese club, including a bilingual
      Studio Ghibli storytime. I play fingerstyle guitar and write songs. I'm also in Toastmasters,
      which has done more for my writing than any style guide.
    </p>

    <h2>Say hello</h2>
    <p>
      If you're building something close to any of this, or you just want to talk about where
      agents break, I'd like to hear from you.
